WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:00.910
RETO MEIER: HI.

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:03.040
I'm Reto Meier, Google
Developer Advocate.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.710
Today, I learned how to get
one terabyte of data processed

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.410
with Google BigQuery
each month at no cost,

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.041
without entering a credit card.

00:00:10.041 --> 00:00:12.924
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.392 --> 00:00:16.600
RETO MEIER: If you're excited
to explore the BigQuery

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.850
public datasets like
these, but you're not

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.530
excited about paying for
it, there's great news.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.850
BigQuery lets you process a
terabyte of data every month

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.260
at no cost.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:27.730
Getting started
is quick and easy,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.751
and you don't even need to
enter your credit card details.

00:00:30.751 --> 00:00:33.250
We'll get started by navigating
the dock to the BigQuery web

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:33.850
UI.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:39.364
Or you can go directly to
BigQuery.cloud.google.com.

00:00:39.364 --> 00:00:42.560
At this point, you'll need to
sign in using a Google account.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.870
Once you're signed in,
continue to BigQuery.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.060
We'll start by
creating a new project.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.590
If it's our first
one, that means

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.230
first reading and accepting the
terms of service for the Google

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.792
Cloud Platform.

00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:55.250
Eventually, we'll want to take
advantage of the free trial.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.260
But let's dismiss it for
now, and create our project.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.920
You can share data and queries
from BigQuery, so make sure

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.530
you give your
project a good name.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.470
Once the project's ready, we'll
get a notification, click that,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.550
and it'll be selected
as our active project.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.170
We're now done, and we
can start running queries.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.810
I'm also going to enable
the BigQuery API here,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.750
so that I can run
programmatic queries later.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.910
Before I run some queries,
let's take a quick peek

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.020
at our billing tab.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.110
As you can see, this
is a new project,

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.190
so there's no billing
instrument associated with it.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.700
Now, when we head back
to the BigQuery web UI,

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.440
we get the BigQuery web UI.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.740
Let's start by querying
the USA names database,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.020
to make sure we can use
that free quota to query

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.790
the public datasets.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.890
We query charges
for queries using

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.500
one metric, the number
of bytes processed,

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.740
rounded to the nearest meg,
with a 10 meg minimum per table,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.830
referenced and data processed.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.330
If you open the
query validator, you

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.940
can see the number of bytes that
will be processed by running

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.650
the query you have entered.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.840
This is calculated as
the total data processed

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.290
for the columns you select, even
if you set an explicit limit.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.060
So it's much more
important to reduce

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.580
the number of columns you
process than the number of rows

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.202
you return.

00:02:06.202 --> 00:02:08.410
When the query returns, the
number of bytes processed

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.419
is displayed here,
and your query log

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.090
shows the exact number
of billable bytes.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.850
Note that you can preview the
full results of every table

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.830
using the Preview
button, so you really

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.260
need to select Star on a table.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.360
When you're comfortable
with using BigQuery,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.710
we can add a billing account.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.050
You still get a free
terabyte each month,

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.749
but you also get to
upload your own data.

00:02:28.749 --> 00:02:30.790
In addition to queries
beyond the first terabyte,

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.090
BigQuery also charges you to
store data and stream inserts.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.550
But loading data, or
copying, exporting,

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.070
or performing metadata
operations like list, update,

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.315
and delete aren't charged.

00:02:41.315 --> 00:02:42.940
If you're new to
Google Cloud platform,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.850
you can take advantage
of the free trial.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.170
It will let you continue
experimenting with BigQuery,

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.930
still at no cost.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.900
Pricing page, here, has
details on exactly how BigQuery

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.560
calculates the
cost of each query,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.485
based on the types of
data in each column.

00:02:55.485 --> 00:02:57.820
Here, I've switched to an
account with billing enabled.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.480
So, I can create a new data set,
and query some of my own data.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.090
Here, I'm using data kept
externally on Google Drive,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.344
rather than storing
it within BigQuery.

00:03:06.344 --> 00:03:08.260
When you need more than
a terabyte in a month,

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.840
you're charged at a rate
of $5.00 per terabyte.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.432
You only pay what
you use, though,

00:03:12.432 --> 00:03:14.140
and it's rounded to
the nearest megabyte.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.230
So that works out to
about half a cent per gig.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.120
And note that you can
set cost controls,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.380
so that once your credit
card has been added,

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.110
you don't have any
unfortunate surprises.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.270
Tune in each week to learn
more BigQuery tips and tricks.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.300
For now, use your
terabyte of processing

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.610
to analyze your own data,
or use our public data

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.862
sets to understand the world
and create cool visualizations.

00:03:35.862 --> 00:03:37.320
Don't forget to
share them with us,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.040
using the
#todayIlearnedwithBigQuery

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.110
hashtag.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.790
And of course,
subscribe to our channel

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.290
and follow our blog to learn
something new with BigQuery.

